  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 PM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nLow pressure continues to churn east across the Central High\nPlains with limited moisture wrapping back into Montana and\nIdaho. We still see a few showers across the eastern highlands\nand the far north end of the Snake Plain. The chance is around\n15% at best, if anything develops other than the current cloud\ncover in that area. Last night, we saw a few pockets of frost,\nfog, and low clouds around central and eastern Idaho. We will\nlikely see that again tonight. We will also see a repeat of\nlocalized light freeze conditions in our colder valleys,\nalthough it will be just a couple of degrees warmer overall.\n\nThe forecast will be dry tomorrow and most of Tuesday, and then\nagain most of Wednesday through Friday morning. In between,\nanother low offshore will shift northeast and across Idaho\nlater Tuesday and Tuesday night. This will bring some light \nrain and likely a few thunderstorms our way. That should be out \nof here earlier on Wednesday and lead to another short dry \nperiod for us. By Friday afternoon and next weekend, a couple of\nstorms will bring some impacts to central and eastern Idaho. \nLow #1 will mainly produce some precipitation over the central \nmountains and along the Montana border, and gusty winds for the \nrest of the area. Low #2 at the moment appears to potentially \nincreasing shower and thunderstorm potential everywhere, \nespecially once we get beyond next weekend. Temperatures will \nbegin to climb throughout the week, with some low to mid 80s \nreturning across our lower valleys. Temperatures quickly back \noff starting Friday, back into the 60s and 70s for valley \nlocations.",
